        ":question/problem": "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $y=2.$ Given that the point $P(1,2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=4{x}^{2}y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(1,2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,2).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2) & =4(1)^{2}(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$"
